  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Less than 15 km from Nice, Mont Chauve d'Aspremont overlooks the Baie des Anges, the Var valley and the surrounding hills. The Alpes-Maritimes Department (Randoxygène) specifies that there are in fact two twin summits, Mont Chauve d'Aspremont to the west (853 m) and the Tourrette one to the east (785 m), both occupied by fortifications and a forest of telecommunications antennas. From the summit viewpoint you can make out Nice harbour, the Baie des Anges, the castle, the airport and the station, with the hilltop village of Falicon directly below; in the distance, the Estérel and the Estéron. The Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office dates the Aspremont fort to 1885-1887; disused, it has been occupied notably by EDF, civil aviation and the fire brigade, and it is not open to visitors.</p>

      <h2>The route</h2>
      <p>The Department describes an 8.4 km tour (+460 m, about 3 hours, medium difficulty) starting from the Aspremont entrance car park (500 m altitude): the Vallière pedestrian path along the Nice road, crossing the road, chemin du Campoun, then the GR5 which climbs in stony zigzags above the village. A long traverse on old terraces leads to the foot of the summit bastion; a DFCI track crosses a small asphalted military road, which climbs in eight hairpins to the fort surrounded by antennas. The return goes by the Baisse de Guigo path (747 m), the Bergerie track, then the Campoun and Vallière paths.</p>
      <p>The Métropole Nice Côte d'Azur, for its part, offers the "P'tite rando à Aspremont": 6.5 km, easy, 2h30, +287 m, starting from the Vigne car park, with commented viewpoints (the hilltop village, the Var and Estéron valleys, the Baie des Anges, and Corsica in clear weather). The figures therefore differ depending on the variant and the source; the principle stays the same: climb above the village, balcony over the Var, fort, return via the Baisse de Guigo.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go with your dog</h2>
      <p>Neither the Department, nor the Métropole, nor the commune publishes a dog rule for this trail: as a precaution, keep your dog on a leash, especially near the fort and on the military road, which is open to vehicles. The route partly uses the GR5, a DFCI track and the access road to the fort, shared with other users. According to a local hiking site, there is very little shade on the loop; so bring water for your dog and set off early in summer.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the summit is very windy, even in fine weather — bring a light layer for yourself and something to shelter your dog from the wind during the picnic stop.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The village of Aspremont, with its stepped lanes typical of the hilltop villages inland from Nice, is easy to visit before or after the hike; the Saint-Claude church is one of the viewpoints on the Métropole circuit. By bus, lines 62 and 76 serve the "Aspremont village" stop.</p>
    </div>

